# Security + cache policy for Cloudflare Pages.
#
# Security headers first (global), then cache rules per path.
# Everything Astro emits under /_astro/ carries a content hash in its filename,
# so a changed file is a changed URL — it can be cached forever and never
# revalidated. The hand-placed assets under /fonts, /logos and /demos are not
# hashed, so they get a long-but-finite TTL that a redeploy can age out.
# HTML is deliberately absent: it must stay revalidated so a deploy is visible
# immediately.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Vary: Accept

# Machine-readable signpost for agents (RFC 8288 Link relations). An agent that
# fetches the homepage learns where the plain-text site summary lives without
# probing well-known paths.
#
# Only resources that actually exist are advertised. An api-catalog and an A2A agent card
# were considered and dropped once: UniverLab was then a static site plus local CLIs, not
# an invocable service, and a Link header pointing at a 404 is worse than no header at
# all. The api-catalog came back the moment the announcements API did — it is a real
# read-only API, so the catalog below describes it. The A2A card stays out: there is still
# no agent to invoke.
/
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain", </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog"; type="application/linkset+json"

/es/
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain", </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog"; type="application/linkset+json"

# The Agent Skills Discovery index and the skills it lists (RFC v0.2.0).
# Served cross-origin on purpose: a registry or an agent running in a browser
# has to be able to read the index from another origin, which is the entire
# point of publishing it. JSON is pinned because Pages would otherwise guess
# from the extension, and SKILL.md files are plain markdown, not downloads.
#
# The two rules must not overlap. Pages applies *every* matching rule and
# concatenates their values rather than letting the more specific one win, so a
# bare `/.well-known/agent-skills/*` also matched the index and produced
# `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8, text/markdown; charset=utf-8`
# — unparseable, and fatal next to `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`. Requiring
# a second path segment keeps the skills rule off the index: every skill file
# lives under its own directory, and the index does not.
/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300

/.well-known/agent-skills/*/*
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300

# RFC 9727 API catalog for the announcements API. The linkset is the only machine-readable
# statement of what the public API is, and whoever fetches it — a registry, an agent —
# is usually on another origin. The media type is pinned because Pages has no mapping for
# an extension-less file and would guess application/octet-stream, and CORS is opened for
# the same reason as the skills index above: a document meant to be read cross-origin.
/.well-known/api-catalog
  Content-Type: application/linkset+json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300

# ARD / AI Catalog capability manifest (specVersion 1.0) — what an agent can use here.
# Same reasoning as the api-catalog above: pinned media type, open CORS, short TTL so a
# renamed document cannot sit in a cache. The path must not overlap the agent-skills
# rules — Pages applies every matching rule and concatenates their values, so a shared
# Content-Type would emit two of them and be unparseable next to nosniff.
/.well-known/ai-catalog.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300

# auth.md — the one file an agent reads before assuming an API needs a key. The media type
# is pinned because Pages has no mapping for `.md` and would serve it as a download, which
# is the exact opposite of what this file is for. No CORS: an agent fetches auth.md from
# the origin it is inspecting, and it is only ever useful there.
/auth.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/logos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/demos/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
